1
00:00:00,404 --> 00:00:05,560
In an old story, Theseus walked into a labyrinth holding a ball of thread,

2
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:08,152
so he could always find his way back.

3
00:00:08,152 --> 00:00:10,378
Depth-first search is that thread.

4
00:00:10,378 --> 00:00:14,714
This maze has seven rooms, joined by twelve one-way doors.

5
00:00:14,714 --> 00:00:19,385
Ada will explore it from room A, unrolling a thread behind her.

6
00:00:20,297 --> 00:00:21,964
Ada carries a clock.

7
00:00:21,964 --> 00:00:25,532
The first time she enters a room, she advances the clock,

8
00:00:25,532 --> 00:00:27,511
and writes the time on the room.

9
00:00:27,511 --> 00:00:31,065
That is the room's starting time, its preorder number.

10
00:00:31,065 --> 00:00:35,453
When she leaves a room for good, because
every door out of it has been tried,

11
00:00:35,453 --> 00:00:40,154
she advances the clock again, and writes that: its finishing time,

12
00:00:40,154 --> 00:00:41,739
its postorder number.

13
00:00:42,638 --> 00:00:46,688
At any moment, every room is in one of three states.

14
00:00:46,688 --> 00:00:49,995
New: Ada has never entered it.

15
00:00:49,995 --> 00:00:54,552
Active: she has entered it, but not yet left it for good.

16
00:00:54,552 --> 00:00:57,235
Finished: she has left it for good.

17
00:00:57,235 --> 00:01:01,595
The active rooms always form one single path, from the start to Ada.

18
00:01:01,595 --> 00:01:03,115
That is the thread.

19
00:01:04,019 --> 00:01:06,519
Ada enters A: clock one.

20
00:01:06,519 --> 00:01:10,738
The door to B leads to a new room, so she goes: clock two.

21
00:01:10,738 --> 00:01:12,925
B to D, clock three.

22
00:01:12,925 --> 00:01:14,970
D to E, clock four.

23
00:01:14,970 --> 00:01:18,095
E has a door back to B, but B is still on the thread.

24
00:01:18,095 --> 00:01:21,871
E has no more doors, so E is finished: clock five.

25
00:01:21,871 --> 00:01:24,905
Back in D: finished, clock six.

26
00:01:24,905 --> 00:01:29,618
Back in B: its other door, to E, leads to a finished room.

27
00:01:29,618 --> 00:01:31,945
B is finished: clock seven.

28
00:01:32,879 --> 00:01:36,994
Back at A, the next door leads to C, a new room:

29
00:01:36,994 --> 00:01:38,153
clock eight.

30
00:01:38,153 --> 00:01:40,184
C to F, nine.

31
00:01:40,184 --> 00:01:43,582
F has a door back to C, which is on the thread.

32
00:01:43,582 --> 00:01:45,887
F to G, ten.

33
00:01:45,887 --> 00:01:51,108
G has a door to D, a finished room, in a branch Ada already completed.

34
00:01:51,108 --> 00:01:53,582
G is finished: eleven.

35
00:01:53,582 --> 00:01:55,679
F is finished: twelve.

36
00:01:55,679 --> 00:02:00,015
Back at C: its door to G leads to a finished room.

37
00:02:00,015 --> 00:02:02,384
C is finished: thirteen.

38
00:02:02,384 --> 00:02:07,476
Back at A: the last door, to E, also leads to a finished room.

39
00:02:07,476 --> 00:02:09,559
A is finished: fourteen.

40
00:02:09,559 --> 00:02:10,948
The walk is over.

41
00:02:11,876 --> 00:02:17,762
Read the rooms by starting time, A, B, D, E, C, F,

42
00:02:17,762 --> 00:02:18,569
G.

43
00:02:18,569 --> 00:02:20,131
That is a preorder.

44
00:02:20,131 --> 00:02:27,110
Read them by finishing time, E, D, B, G, F, C, A.

45
00:02:27,110 --> 00:02:28,699
That is a postorder.

46
00:02:28,699 --> 00:02:32,228
With a different order of doors, or a different starting room,

47
00:02:32,228 --> 00:02:33,813
you would get different ones.

48
00:02:34,723 --> 00:02:39,059
Draw each room as a bar, from its starting time to its finishing time.

49
00:02:39,059 --> 00:02:42,809
Any two bars are either nested, one inside the other,

50
00:02:42,809 --> 00:02:44,515
or completely apart.

51
00:02:44,515 --> 00:02:50,166
And one room's bar contains another's exactly
when it is an ancestor in the thread tree.

52
00:02:50,166 --> 00:02:54,068
The bar for room B contains the bars for D and E.

53
00:02:54,989 --> 00:02:58,010
The book says this in four equivalent ways.

54
00:02:58,010 --> 00:03:02,476
One: u is an ancestor of v in the thread tree.

55
00:03:02,476 --> 00:03:05,901
Two: u's bar contains v's bar.

56
00:03:05,901 --> 00:03:08,895
Three: when v starts, u is active.

57
00:03:08,895 --> 00:03:14,820
Four: just before u starts, there is a path
from u to v through rooms that are all new.

58
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:16,769
All four mean the same thing.

59
00:03:17,703 --> 00:03:19,786
Now classify the doors.

60
00:03:19,786 --> 00:03:23,628
When Ada tries a door from u to v, there are four cases,

61
00:03:23,628 --> 00:03:25,919
depending on v's state at that moment.

62
00:03:25,919 --> 00:03:29,109
If v is new, it is a tree edge.

63
00:03:29,109 --> 00:03:31,674
If v is active, it is a back edge.

64
00:03:31,674 --> 00:03:35,620
If v is finished and below u, it is a forward edge.

65
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:39,275
If v is finished, and somewhere else, it is a cross edge.

66
00:03:40,188 --> 00:03:46,347
A tree edge: v is new, so Ada walks
through, and the door joins the thread tree.

67
00:03:46,347 --> 00:03:48,899
A to B is a tree edge.

68
00:03:48,899 --> 00:03:50,575
So are five more.

69
00:03:51,491 --> 00:03:55,489
A back edge: v is active, already on the thread.

70
00:03:55,489 --> 00:03:59,785
E to B: B is on the thread, so this door loops back up it.

71
00:03:59,785 --> 00:04:01,804
F to C does the same.

72
00:04:01,804 --> 00:04:04,001
Two doors are back edges.

73
00:04:04,954 --> 00:04:08,340
A forward edge: v is finished, and it is below u.

74
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:11,738
B to E: Ada already reached E through D.

75
00:04:11,738 --> 00:04:14,655
C to G and A to E are the same.

76
00:04:14,655 --> 00:04:16,904
Three doors are forward edges.

77
00:04:17,828 --> 00:04:21,474
A cross edge: v is finished, and not below u.

78
00:04:21,474 --> 00:04:27,919
G to D: D sits in a branch that Ada completed
before she even entered G's branch.

79
00:04:27,919 --> 00:04:30,207
One door is a cross edge.

80
00:04:31,082 --> 00:04:32,775
What do the bars look like?

81
00:04:32,775 --> 00:04:36,473
For a tree edge, u's bar contains v's.

82
00:04:36,473 --> 00:04:38,582
For a forward edge, the same.

83
00:04:38,582 --> 00:04:42,007
For a back edge, v's bar contains u's.

84
00:04:42,007 --> 00:04:46,447
For a cross edge, v's bar is entirely before u's.

85
00:04:46,447 --> 00:04:49,754
A fifth kind, with v starting after u has finished,

86
00:04:49,754 --> 00:04:54,555
is impossible, because Ada tries every door out of u before she leaves it.

87
00:04:55,493 --> 00:04:57,707
Here is the key fact.

88
00:04:57,707 --> 00:05:02,264
If any door leads to an active room, the maze has a cycle.

89
00:05:02,264 --> 00:05:07,420
The thread runs from B down to E, and
the door from E back to B closes the loop.

90
00:05:07,420 --> 00:05:10,776
But doors to finished rooms never prove a cycle.

91
00:05:11,662 --> 00:05:15,021
There is a second way to see it, with finishing times.

92
00:05:15,021 --> 00:05:18,876
Here is every door, with the two finishing times.

93
00:05:18,876 --> 00:05:23,277
For a back edge, the room it points to is still active when u finishes,

94
00:05:23,277 --> 00:05:24,930
so it finishes later.

95
00:05:24,930 --> 00:05:29,592
If an edge u to v has u finishing before v, there is a cycle.

96
00:05:29,592 --> 00:05:33,677
In a DAG, every edge has u finishing after v.

97
00:05:34,607 --> 00:05:36,912
Now the algorithm, in Python.

98
00:05:36,912 --> 00:05:39,594
It keeps a status for every room.

99
00:05:39,594 --> 00:05:41,977
First, an empty dictionary.

100
00:05:41,977 --> 00:05:44,825
A loop sets every room's status to new.

101
00:05:45,703 --> 00:05:49,349
Visit is a function inside a function, one call per room.

102
00:05:49,349 --> 00:05:53,659
The moment Ada enters v, its status becomes active.

103
00:05:53,659 --> 00:05:59,319
Then, for each door out of v, to a room w, look at w's status.

104
00:06:00,235 --> 00:06:03,438
If w is active, that is a back edge.

105
00:06:03,438 --> 00:06:06,238
Return False: there is a cycle.

106
00:06:06,238 --> 00:06:10,157
If w is new, that is a tree edge, so visit it.

107
00:06:10,157 --> 00:06:13,907
And if that visit comes back False, a cycle was found deeper down,

108
00:06:13,907 --> 00:06:15,691
so return False too.

109
00:06:15,691 --> 00:06:20,297
If w is finished, do nothing: it can never prove a cycle.

110
00:06:21,221 --> 00:06:25,648
When every door out of v has been tried, v becomes finished.

111
00:06:25,648 --> 00:06:30,592
And visit returns True: no cycle was found at or below this room.

112
00:06:31,503 --> 00:06:32,792
The outer loop.

113
00:06:32,792 --> 00:06:35,631
Try every room as a starting point.

114
00:06:35,631 --> 00:06:38,717
Skip the ones an earlier walk already covered.

115
00:06:38,717 --> 00:06:42,271
If any walk finds a cycle, the answer is False.

116
00:06:42,271 --> 00:06:45,670
If every room is covered and no back edge was found,

117
00:06:45,670 --> 00:06:48,934
return True: the graph is a DAG.

118
00:06:49,809 --> 00:06:51,307
Run it on the maze.

119
00:06:51,307 --> 00:06:53,443
Visit A: active.

120
00:06:53,443 --> 00:06:54,602
Visit B.

121
00:06:54,602 --> 00:06:55,500
Visit D.

122
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:56,672
Visit E.

123
00:06:56,672 --> 00:07:00,943
Now E's door leads to B, and B's status is active.

124
00:07:00,943 --> 00:07:04,758
Return False, right away: the maze has a cycle.

125
00:07:04,758 --> 00:07:07,046
It never even looks at the rest.

126
00:07:07,916 --> 00:07:11,809
Remove the two back edges, E to B and F to C.

127
00:07:11,809 --> 00:07:13,398
Ten doors are left.

128
00:07:13,398 --> 00:07:18,216
Now no door ever leads to an active room, and the function returns True.

129
00:07:18,216 --> 00:07:19,973
This is a DAG.

130
00:07:19,973 --> 00:07:23,958
A room with no doors in is a source: here, room A.

131
00:07:23,958 --> 00:07:28,229
A room with no doors out is a sink: here, room E.

132
00:07:28,229 --> 00:07:32,561
Every DAG has at least one source and one sink.

133
00:07:33,462 --> 00:07:34,868
One more detail.

134
00:07:34,868 --> 00:07:39,986
If we start a walk from every room in
turn, skipping the ones already entered,

135
00:07:39,986 --> 00:07:42,720
the number of separate trees depends on the order.

136
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:45,298
Start at A: one tree.

137
00:07:45,298 --> 00:07:48,970
Start at G first, and the first walk is stuck after G,

138
00:07:48,970 --> 00:07:50,596
D, E, B.

139
00:07:50,596 --> 00:07:52,954
Then A starts a second tree.

140
00:07:52,954 --> 00:07:58,367
In a directed graph, even a connected one,
the count can be anything from one to V.

141
00:07:59,287 --> 00:08:00,459
To recap.

142
00:08:00,459 --> 00:08:03,623
Every room gets two stamps from one clock.

143
00:08:03,623 --> 00:08:08,597
Every room is new, active, or finished, and the active rooms are the thread.

144
00:08:08,597 --> 00:08:13,037
Every door is a tree, forward, back, or cross edge.

145
00:08:13,037 --> 00:08:17,230
A back edge, an edge to an active room, means a cycle.

146
00:08:17,230 --> 00:08:20,732
Next: topological sort, and dynamic programming,

147
00:08:20,732 --> 00:08:24,153
sections six point three and six point four.
